  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Consider any way to pair the integers 111 through 2N2N2N into NNN pairs (A1,B1),(A2,B2),…,(AN,BN)(A_1,B_1), (A_2,B_2), \\ldots, (A_N,B_N)(A1​,B1​),(A2​,B2​),…,(AN​,BN​) in such a way that each of these integers is present in exactly one pair, Ai&lt;BiA_i \\lt B_iAi​&lt;Bi​ for each valid iii and A1&lt;A2&lt;…&lt;ANA_1 \\lt A_2 \\lt \\ldots \\lt A_NA1​&lt;A2​&lt;…&lt;AN​. Let's call such a sequence of pairs a pairing.\n\nFor a pairing (A1,B1),(A2,B2),…,(AN,BN)(A_1,B_1), (A_2,B_2), \\ldots, (A_N,B_N)(A1​,B1​),(A2​,B2​),…,(AN​,BN​), we can create an undirected graph with 2N2N2N nodes (numbered 111 through 2N2N2N) and the following edges:\n\n\nfirst type: for each valid iii, an edge between nodes iii and i+1i+1i+1\nsecond type: for each valid iii, an edge between nodes AiA_iAi​ and BiB_iBi​\nThis pairing is valid if for each edge (i,i+1)(i,i+1)(i,i+1) of the first type, there is an edge of the second type (Aj,Bj)(A_j, B_j)(Aj​,Bj​) which \"surrounds\" it, i.e. satisfies Aj≤iA_j \\le iAj​≤i and Bj&gt;iB_j \\gt iBj​&gt;i. The cost of a valid pairing is defined as the number of pairs of edges of the second type such that one of these edges is \"nested\" in the other, i.e. pairs (Ai,Bi)(A_i, B_i)(Ai​,Bi​) and (Aj,Bj)(A_j, B_j)(Aj​,Bj​) such that Ai&lt;Aj&lt;Bj&lt;BiA_i \\lt A_j \\lt B_j \\lt B_iAi​&lt;Aj​&lt;Bj​&lt;Bi​.\n\nFind the sum of costs of all valid pairings. Since this number may be very large, compute it modulo a given prime PPP.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1021 \\le T \\le 10^21≤T≤102",
      "2≤N≤1032 \\le N \\le 10^32≤N≤103",
      "1≤P≤1091 \\le P \\le 10^91≤P≤109",
      "PPP is prime",
      "the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10,00010,00010,000",
      "Subtasks**Subtask #1 (50 points):** N≤10N \\le 10N≤10"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and PPP.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer — the sum of costs of all valid pairings modulo PPP.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2 17\n3 5\n817 999999797",
        "output": "1\n3\n223160771",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1: For N=2N = 2N=2, there are two valid pairings [(1,3),(2,4)][(1,3), (2,4)][(1,3),(2,4)] and [(1,4),(2,3)][(1,4), (2,3)][(1,4),(2,3)], with costs 000 and 111 respectively.\nExample case 2: For N=3N = 3N=3, all valid pairings along with their costs are listed below.\n\n[(1,4),(2,5),(3,6)][(1,4), (2,5), (3,6)][(1,4),(2,5),(3,6)] with cost 000\n[(1,4),(2,6),(3,5)][(1,4), (2,6), (3,5)][(1,4),(2,6),(3,5)] with cost 111\n[(1,5),(2,4),(3,6)][(1,5), (2,4), (3,6)][(1,5),(2,4),(3,6)] with cost 111\n[(1,5),(2,6),(3,4)][(1,5), (2,6), (3,4)][(1,5),(2,6),(3,4)] with cost 222\n[(1,6),(2,5),(3,4)][(1,6), (2,5), (3,4)][(1,6),(2,5),(3,4)] with cost 333\n[(1,6),(2,4),(3,5)][(1,6), (2,4), (3,5)][(1,6),(2,4),(3,5)] with cost 222\n[(1,3),(2,6),(4,5)][(1,3), (2,6), (4,5)][(1,3),(2,6),(4,5)] with cost 111\n[(1,3),(2,5),(4,6)][(1,3), (2,5), (4,6)][(1,3),(2,5),(4,6)] with cost 000\n[(1,6),(2,3),(4,5)][(1,6), (2,3), (4,5)][(1,6),(2,3),(4,5)] with cost 222\n[(1,5),(2,3),(4,6)][(1,5), (2,3), (4,6)][(1,5),(2,3),(4,6)] with cost 111\n\nThe sum of all costs is 131313 and its remainder modulo 555 is 333."
      }
    ]
